from pathlib import Path
import re
p=Path("_site/index.html"); html=p.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
add='''<section class="section recovered-story" id="recovered-mechanics"><div class="wrap narrow"><div class="chapter-marker"><span class="chapter-index">2005–2006</span><span class="chapter-line"></span><span>THE NUMBERS WE WERE TESTING</span></div><h2>One number could change everything.</h2><p>The old Funky PI discussions included real test figures. Players discussed <strong>1,572,873 mana with level 51 Telekinesis</strong>, and another suggested testing <strong>2,621,441 or 2,621,442 mana with level 50, 70, or 99 Telekinesis</strong>. These weren't guaranteed formulas for an invincible character. They were examples people tested, challenged, and adjusted around regeneration, Prayer, resistance, and other defensive effects.</p><p>The one-hit Stun discussion supplied an exact trigger: <strong>90% chance to cast level 10 Stun on attack</strong> and <strong>90% chance to cast level 10 Stun on hit</strong>, with other attack and hit effects removed. Neounk_BoT explained that the repeated triggering could kill quickly, but reflected Thorns could kill the attacker too. The replies immediately turned to balancing Smite damage and protection against the return damage.</p><p>And a September 20, 2006 reply from <strong>PIB-Xenom666</strong> described a <strong>127% chance to reanimate a “sucker” monster on kill</strong>, along with monster resistance and an all-resistance aura, to attack a Funky PI character. Finding that strange counter written down by another player brought back the flying-monster experiments I remembered.</p><p class="archive-links"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011215530/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?showtopic=1372" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Funky PI values ↗</a> <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?showtopic=15079" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stun discovery ↗</a> <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011215449/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?showtopic=10321" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sucker counter ↗</a></p></div></section>'''
if 'id="recovered-mechanics"' not in html:
  mark='id="research-debates-2006"'; ix=html.find(mark)
  if ix<0:raise SystemExit("research debate section missing")
  pos=html.rfind("<section",0,ix)
  html=html[:pos]+add+html[pos:]
ce='''<section class="section recovered-story" id="cheat-engine-trust"><div class="wrap narrow"><div class="chapter-marker"><span class="chapter-index">2006</span><span class="chapter-line"></span><span>WHEN TRUST STARTED TO BREAK</span></div><h2>What if the host controlled the fight?</h2><p>A September 2006 forum thread on Cheat Engine counters showed players arguing over whether memory-locked characters could be beaten with old 1.09 builds, careful Smite damage, or Life Tap. Others challenged the experiments, explaining that freezing the wrong memory values could give a false sense of invulnerability. There were disagreements about what worked for hosts and joiners and about the differences between Open and Closed Battle.net.</p><p>xRiCx_HoS praised the exchange of tricks and counters and pinned the discussion. But by November, BlAzEn was describing how much power an experienced host could exercise with a memory editor, and how that was making public games harder to trust. He wanted people to stop relying on programs and get back to proper duels.</p><p>That is what makes the thread linger with me. We built our competition around discoveries. When a player couldn't be sure whether a host was secretly changing the game, the competition itself began to lose its meaning.</p><p class="archive-links"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?showtopic=10162" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original CE counter debate ↗</a></p></div></section>'''
if 'id="cheat-engine-trust"' not in html:
  mark='id="legacy"'; ix=html.find(mark)
  if ix<0:raise SystemExit("legacy anchor missing")
  pos=html.rfind("<section",0,ix)
  html=html[:pos]+ce+html[pos:]
p.write_text(html,encoding="utf-8")
js=Path("_site/script.js"); text=js.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
old="I had forgotten the exact setup until I found an old forum reply describing it: 90% chance to cast level 10 Stun on attack and on hit. That was how we triggered the rapid attacks."
new="Neounk_BoT documented the setup in December 2006: 90% chance to cast level 10 Stun on attack and 90% chance to cast level 10 Stun on hit, with other on-attack and on-hit skills removed. Repeating attacks could kill the victim quickly, but Thorns damage could kill the attacker too."
if old in text:text=text.replace(old,new)
# Correct the original source line if previous patch was bypassed
text=text.replace("An NV stun-on-hit property reportedly produced a glitch-like sequence of extremely rapid attacks.",new)
js.write_text(text,encoding="utf-8")
